---
import { faqText } from '../../../lib/seo';
import Preview from '../../../components/site/Preview.astro';
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ScheduleMaker from '../../../components/ScheduleMaker.tsx';
import Faqs from '../../../components/homeschool/Faqs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../../config';

const url = new URL('/preschool-schedule/bedtime-routine-chart/', SITE.url).href;
const title = `Bedtime Routine Chart: Free Printable with Pictures | ${SITE.name}`;
const description = 'Make a free printable bedtime routine chart with pictures and your child’s name: bath, pajamas, potty, teeth, story, hugs and lights out, or cut-apart cards.';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What should be on a bedtime routine chart?', a: 'The few steps your child does every night, in the order you do them. For many preschoolers that is bath (on bath nights), pajamas, potty, brush teeth, a story, a hug and lights out. Five to eight steps is plenty. If a step only happens some nights, leave it off or make a card for it.' },
  { q: 'How long should a bedtime routine take?', a: 'Many families aim for about 20 to 30 minutes from pajamas to lights out, a little longer on bath nights. What helps most is doing the same steps in the same order at about the same time each night, more than the exact length.' },
  { q: 'At what age can a child use a bedtime routine chart?', a: 'Many kids start following a picture chart somewhere between 2 and 3, with a grown-up pointing at each step. By 4 or 5, some kids can walk through it with only a reminder. Kids vary a lot here, and that’s normal.' },
  { q: 'How much sleep does a preschooler need?', a: 'The American Academy of Sleep Medicine recommends 10 to 13 hours in 24 hours, naps included, for children 3 to 5, and 9 to 12 hours for children 6 to 12. If you have concerns about your child’s sleep, ask your child’s pediatrician.' },
  { q: 'Is this bedtime routine chart printable free?', a: 'Yes. The chart, the cut-apart cards, every picture and your own words are free, one page at a time. Members can also print the page for every child on their list at once, or both pages in one PDF, with no footer line.', html: 'Yes. The chart, the cut-apart cards, every picture and your own words are free, one page at a time. <a href="/membership/">Members</a> can also print the page for every child on their list at once, or both pages in one PDF, with no footer line.' },
  { q: 'Is my child’s name sent anywhere?', a: 'No. The chart is drawn in your browser, with the name and any words you type. They stay on your device and are never sent to our server or put in a link.' },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'LearningResource',
    name: 'Bedtime routine chart maker (free printable with pictures)',
    url,
    learningResourceType: 'Printable chart',
    educationalLevel: 'Toddler, Preschool, Kindergarten',
    teaches: 'The bedtime routine in order, with a picture for each step',
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
    description,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE.url + '/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Preschool schedule', item: SITE.url + '/preschool-schedule/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Bedtime routine chart', item: url },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: faqText(f) } })),
  },
];
---
<Base title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="pt-6 text-[14px] text-muted">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-ink">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> <a href="/preschool-schedule/" class="hover:text-ink">Preschool schedule</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> <span aria-current="page">Bedtime routine chart</span>
    </nav>
    <header class="pt-6 pb-8 max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] sm:text-[2.8rem] font-bold"><span class="ruled">Printable bedtime routine chart</span></h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted">This page makes a free printable bedtime routine chart with pictures: each step from bath to lights out, with a box to check off, or a page of cut-apart picture cards. Change the steps and words to match your night, add your child's name, and print.</p>
    </header>

    <div id="maker" class="scroll-mt-24">
      <ScheduleMaker client:load source="bedtime-routine-chart" preset="bedtime" kinds={['day', 'cards']} />
    </div>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-8">
      <Preview k="bedtime-routine-chart" caption="Example: bedtime routine chart with seven steps." />
      <Preview k="bedtime-routine-cards" caption="Example: bedtime picture cards to cut apart." />
    </div>

    <article class="prose-tl max-w-[68ch] mt-14">
      <p class="text-[1.15rem]!">
        Bedtime goes smoother when a child knows exactly what comes next. A bedtime routine chart puts the steps in pictures, so a
        3 or 4 year old can see that pajamas come before teeth and that one story comes before lights out. The chart becomes the one
        giving directions, and you get to be the one cheering.
      </p>

      <h2>Two bedtime printables</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bedtime routine chart:</strong> up to 12 steps in order, each with a picture, your words, an optional time and a box to check off. Slip it in a sheet protector and use a dry-erase marker to check the boxes again each night.</li>
        <li><strong>Bedtime picture cards:</strong> 12 cards with dashed cut lines. Stick them in a row on the bedroom door and let your child turn each one over or move it to a "done" pocket. Empty cards print blank to draw on.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        There are bedtime pictures for bath, pajamas, potty, wash hands, wash face, brush teeth, a drink of water, clothes for tomorrow,
        story time, hugs and kisses, the night light and lights out, plus all the daytime pictures from
        our <a href="/preschool-schedule/">preschool daily schedule maker</a>. Change any label to your family's words, like "Bath (Mon, Wed, Fri)" or "Two songs".
      </p>

      <h2>A simple bedtime routine for a preschooler</h2>
      <p>
        The "Bedtime routine" sample in the maker is a calm 20 to 30 minute routine, a little longer on bath nights. It runs from the
        busiest step to the quietest:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>6:45, bath:</strong> warm water and a few minutes of play. On non-bath nights, wash hands and face instead.</li>
        <li><strong>Pajamas and potty:</strong> let your child choose between two pairs of pajamas. A small choice helps them feel in charge.</li>
        <li><strong>Brush teeth:</strong> a grown-up usually helps at this age. Many families brush for about two minutes with a song.</li>
        <li><strong>7:05, story time:</strong> one or two books in bed, chosen before you start, so "one more" has an easy answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Hugs and kisses, then 7:30, lights out:</strong> the same short goodnight words every night, then leave the room.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Times are only a guide, so shift the whole thing to fit your evening. Most preschoolers can't read a clock yet, and the order is
        what they follow. On nights without a bath, or when the evening runs late, try the "Short bedtime (no bath)" sample instead.
      </p>

      <h2>Bedtime routine chart for toddlers</h2>
      <p>
        Younger kids do best with fewer, bigger pictures. For a 2 year old, try three to five steps (pajamas, teeth, story, lights out)
        and the picture cards rather than the list. Hold up each card as you start that step, and say its name. Toddlers often like
        handing you the card when the step is done, which turns the routine into a small game instead of a string of instructions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Older preschoolers and kindergartners can usually handle the full chart and check off the boxes themselves. Some like adding a
        step of their own, like setting out clothes for tomorrow.
      </p>

      <h2>How to make the chart stick</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Make it together.</strong> Let your child help pick the steps, put the cards in order or color the pictures. Kids tend to follow a plan they helped make.</li>
        <li><strong>Hang it where bedtime happens.</strong> The bathroom door or the bedroom wall at your child's eye level works well.</li>
        <li><strong>Let the chart say no.</strong> When the request for a third story comes, point at the chart: "Story time is done. What's next?"</li>
        <li><strong>Keep it the same for two weeks</strong> before changing anything, then change one step at a time.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Every child settles differently, and it can take a few weeks before a new routine feels normal. If bedtime stays very hard, or
        you have concerns about your child's sleep, ask your child's pediatrician.
      </p>

      <h2>The rest of the day</h2>
      <p>
        The same pictures make a daytime schedule too. Print a <a href="/preschool-schedule/">picture schedule for the day</a> for the
        fridge, a <a href="/preschool-schedule/morning-routine-chart/">morning routine chart</a> for getting out the door, and read our <a href="/learn/preschool-homeschool-schedule/">preschool homeschool schedule guide</a> for a sample day
        and week, with a short learning time and a calm wind-down in the evening.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you're teaching at home, our <a href="/homeschool/preschool/">homeschool preschool curriculum</a> lays out 36 weeks with a
        letter and a number each week, about 15 minutes a day. Week 1 is
        free: <a href="/program/?level=pre-k#placement">take the free placement check</a> to start at the right week.
      </p>

      <h2>Try this tonight</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Before dinner, 5 minutes:</strong> print the chart with the "Bedtime routine" sample and your child's name.</li>
        <li><strong>After dinner:</strong> read it together from the top, pointing at each picture, and tape it up where bedtime starts.</li>
        <li><strong>At each step:</strong> walk back to the chart and let your child check the box.</li>
        <li><strong>Tomorrow morning:</strong> notice which step was hardest, and change only that one for the next night.</li>
      </ol>
    </article>

    <section class="prose-tl max-w-[68ch] mt-14" aria-labelledby="faq-h">
      <h2 id="faq-h">Questions about bedtime routine charts</h2>
      <Faqs faqs={faqs} />
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
